\begin_body

\begin_layout Title
Poverty Mapping in the Age of Machine Learning
\end_layout

\begin_layout Abstract
\begin_inset Newpage pagebreak
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Introduction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Methodological advances, the availability of satellite derived data as well
 as a litany of other sources of data have led to considerable innovations
 in the area of poverty mapping.
 Machine learning approaches coupled with these innovative data sources
 are now often used to yield a granular picture of poverty across the globe,
 and many of these poverty maps feed into policy making.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Poverty maps are attractive since they are a useful way to illustrate poverty
 estimates at a granular local level.
 These estimates are then used by policy makers to determine where the limited
 funds at their disposal should be allocated.
 However, estimates of poverty at the local level which are sufficiently
 precise to minimize errors of inclusion and exclusion are not always readily
 available.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Household surveys measuring living standards provide population statistics,
 like poverty rates or mean expenditures, for a given country.
 Such household surveys may also provide estimates that are sufficiently
 reliable for large areas in a given country.
 However, household surveys suffer from three flaws which hamper poverty
 monitoring and alleviation efforts at a granular level.
 The first flaw is related to time.
 Household surveys in many developing countries are conducted infrequently,
 in some cases more than a decade may pass before a new survey is implemented.
 A second drawback of household surveys for the purposes of poverty monitoring
 at the local level is that household surveys can only be disaggregated
 up to a certain point before the estimates lose validity (i.e.
 become too noisy).
 The third, and final drawback, of household surveys is that these surveys
 may not cover all the locations of interest to policy makers who are trying
 to determine which areas should be prioritized.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The drawbacks related to disaggregation and coverage are often addressed
 through small area estimation.
 Small area estimation is a branch of statistics focused on obtaining estimates
 of higher quality than those obtained directly from the household survey.
 These small area estimation techniques often combine data from household
 surveys and auxiliary information from censuses, registers, or others to
 produce estimates for areas with small sample sizes of higher quality than
 is possible from survey data alone.
 Since there is no such thing as a free lunch, to achieve these gains in
 quality it is necessary to rely on model assumptions which must be thoroughly
 checked.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the context of poverty, small area estimation can be split into unit-level
 models and area-level models.
 Unit-level models for small area estimation will often yield the largest
 gains in quality.
 Under unit-level models a linear relationship is assumed between welfare
 and a set of household and individual level characteristics.
 The parameters obtained from such a linear relationship are used to simulate
 welfare for every unit in the census data.
 From the simulated welfare vector it is possible to obtain poverty headcount
 rates and other indicators.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
For a thorough discussion on small area estimation for poverty headcount
 rates see 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "sae_guide"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "mcn2021"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 For a thorough discussion on small area estimation see 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "rao2015small"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 Perhaps the first unit-level model for small area estimation of poverty
 indicators was proposed by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "jesko1998"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 which was later refined by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "elbers2003micro"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, this method quickly became popular across the globe and is perhaps the
 most applied methodology for poverty mapping.
 The empirical best (EB) method for poverty mapping was introduced by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "molina2010small"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 which relies on similar model assumptions to ELL but due to EB, which makes
 use of the survey data available for areas present in the survey, yields
 estimates with considerably greater precision than ELL and represents a
 significant upgrade as noted in validations with simulated and real world
 data (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citealt
key "corral2021map"
literal "false"

\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The drawback with unit-level models is that they rely on covariates that
 should have similar moments of the distribution across the data used, otherwise
 estimates will tend to be biased.
 This is most likely to occur when census data and survey data correspond
 to different years, but it may also occur in other instances where the
 data are misaligned.
 This represents a limitation to the usefulness of unit-level small area
 estimates since in most developing countries census data is collected once
 every ten years and survey data may not be contemporaneous.
 When unit-level models are not possible there is no fully vetted methodology
 that may yield sufficiently precise small area estimates of poverty.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Traditionally in off-census years area-level models such as the one proposed
 by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "fay1979estimates"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 and sub-area-level models proposed by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "torabi2014small"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 are the go to methods.
 Area-level models rely on aggregate data at the area level and consequently
 do not require contemporary census data to obtain small area estimates.
 Despite their desirable features, the gains in precision offered by these
 models is quite limited (see 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "molina_morales2009"
literal "false"

\end_inset

).
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Considerable efforts have been devoted to methods which can be applied for
 poverty mapping in off-census years.
 One proposed method attempts to combine the best features of unit-level
 models and area-level models (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citealt
key "doi:10.1080/00220388.2012.682983,lange2018small,masaki2020small"
literal "false"

\end_inset

), called unit-context models.
 Specifically, the method models household level welfare from a household
 survey using only aggregate data.
 Aggregate data may come from a recent or previous census, administrative
 data, or GIS data.
 The latter data have become a selling point of many recent poverty maps,
 particularly poverty maps with estimates obtained from machine learning
 methods.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Poverty maps constructed from machine learning methods have gained popularity
 in academic and policy circles.
 Although these machine learning based estimates are often called poverty
 maps, in reality these often obtain estimates of an asset index.
 Although highly correlated to poverty, asset indexes are not the same as
 a welfare aggregate (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citealt
key "FilmerScott"
literal "false"

\end_inset

).
 Despite this, the methods have become very popular and during the COVID-19
 pandemic were applied by governments desperately in need for any sort of
 relevant information to assist their targeting efforts to reach those most
 in need.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
See 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "vishwanath"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 for an application in Nigeria.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
With this backdrop, the present research builds upon the work from 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "corral2021map"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 who validate small area estimation models using the Mexican Intra-censal
 survey data.
 Specifically, this paper adds to the validation exercise by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "corral2021map"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 by including machine learning based estimates of poverty.
 The goal is to compare the quality of machine learning based estimates
 of monetary head count poverty for small areas to traditional small area
 based estimates of monetary head count poverty in a scenario where every
 method is applied to the same data.
 As far as we are aware this is the first validation of machine learning
 based methods where the quality of the estimates are measured against other
 estimation methods and where the true poverty rate for each small area
 domain is known, and thus yields empirical measures of design based bias
 and mean squared errors.
 This, in our view, is also a much more thorough validation approach than
 the one often used in the literature which often relies on aggregate measures
 such as a coefficient of determination.
 In section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:Validation-methods-for"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

 this is discussed and we suggest the potential flaws of relying on such
 a measure.
\end_layout

