\begin_layout Section
Methods and validation approach
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This section provides a brief overview of the methods applied.
 The section first gives an overview of gradient boosting, since the method
 is popular for machine learning based poverty mapping.
 Then a brief discussion on previously implemented validation approaches
 for machine learning based estimates is discussed.
 Unit-level methods that combine survey and census data to obtain poverty
 estimates are then discussed.
 Also, since the reason for obtaining highly disaggregated poverty estimates
 is to improve the targeting of resources, the methods are compared by their
 prowess for poverty reduction using the Mexican Intra-censal survey.
 The targeting simulation presented builds upon the simulation presented
 by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "elbers2007poverty"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 with one key difference: because the true poverty rates for each small
 area are known, the efficiency yielded by the use of different methods
 is compared to an optimal level of efficiency.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Note Note
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
The section highlights how small area estimation based poverty estimates
 differ from machine learning based poverty estimates.
 
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Machine learning
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The goal of machine learning is to develop high-performance algorithms for
 prediction, classification, and clustering/grouping tasks 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "varian2014big,athey2018impact,athey2019machine"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Such tasks can be divided into supervised and unsupervised learning problems.
 While unsupervised learning focuses on identifying clusters of observations
 that are similar in terms of their features, supervised learning uses a
 set of features to predict some outcome of interest.
 Supervised learning can be further divided into regression and classification
 tasks, where regression is concerned with predicting continuous outcomes
 and classification focuses on categorical outcomes.
 While there are many machine-learning methods used for regression and classific
ation tasks (e.g., lasso, random forests, or support vector machines), here
 we fix ideas by focusing on a method that has been particularly popular
 for poverty mapping: gradient boosting.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Originally developed by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "friedman2001greedy"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, gradient boosting machines are a family of machine-learning techniques
 that combine a large number of weak learners to form a stronger ensemble
 prediction.
 Unlike some common ensemble techniques (e.g., random forests) that simply
 average models in the ensemble, gradient boosting adds models to the ensemble
 sequentially by fitting new weak learners to the negative gradient of some
 chosen loss function.
 With the classic squared-error loss function, this amounts to sequentially
 fitting new models to the current residuals of the ensemble.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
See 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "natekin2013gradient"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 for an accessible introduction to gradient boosting.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 Extreme gradient boosting (EGB) is a particularly popular implementation
 of gradient boosting that uses classification and regression trees as the
 base models or weak learners.
 The popularity of EGB is due to the fact that it is fast, scalable, and
 has been shown to outperform competing methods across a wide range of problems
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "chen2016xgboost"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Classification and regression trees are based on a sequential binary splitting
 of the covariate space that serves to partition the data into subsamples
 that are increasingly homogeneous in terms of the outcome variable.
 A tree begins with a single root node that is split into two child nodes
 according to some decision rule.
 A decision rule pertains to a single explanatory variable and observations
 are assigned to the child nodes depending on whether they meet some condition
 associated with the explanatory variable.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
For example, for a continuous explanatory variable, one determines whether
 a given observations falls above or below some numeric cutoff.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 The child nodes can be further split into new child nodes based on different
 decision rules that involve other explanatory variables and cutoff points.
 Any child node that is not further split is referred to as a terminal node
 or leaf, and each leaf is associated with a parameter that provides a predictio
n for the subsample associated with that leaf.
 For any given observation, the ensemble prediction used by EGB is the sum
 of that observation's predictions across all trees in the ensemble.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In what follows, we discuss the key features of the EGB algorithm, drawing
 on the presentation provided in 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "chen2016xgboost"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

 denote the outcome of interest (e.g., poverty headcounts) for observation
 or entity 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

, and let 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 denote a vector of explanatory variables.
 In line with the above, the predicted value of the outcome 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{y}_{i}$
\end_inset

 is given by the sum of predictions across the individual trees in the ensemble:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{y}_{i}=\sum_{t}f_{t}(x_{i})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where the trees are indexed by 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{t}(x_{i})$
\end_inset

 gives the prediction of a given tree as a function of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 To build the trees used in the model, EGB minimizes the following regularized
 objective function:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}=\sum_{i}l(y_{i},\hat{y}_{i})+\sum_{t}r(f_{t})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $l(y_{i},\hat{y}_{i})$
\end_inset

 is the loss associated with the 
\begin_inset Formula $i^{th}$
\end_inset

 observation and 
\begin_inset Formula $r(f_{t})$
\end_inset

 is a regularization term that serves to mitigate overfitting.
 In our implementation, we use the classic squared-error loss such that
 
\begin_inset Formula $l(y_{i},\hat{y}_{i})=(y_{i}-\hat{y}_{i})^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
EGB uses the following specification of the regularization term: 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
r(f_{t})=\gamma M_{t}+\frac{1}{2}\lambda\sum_{j}m_{tj}^{2}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $M_{t}$
\end_inset

 is the total number of leaves in tree 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $m_{tj}$
\end_inset

 is the prediction associated with the 
\begin_inset Formula $j^{th}$
\end_inset

 leaf in a given tree, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset

 are hyperparameters that must be chosen by the user (we discuss our selection
 of hyperparameters below).
 The regularization term serves to mitigate overfitting by penalizing complicate
d trees and smoothing the predictions associated with each tree.
 That is, the method relies on a large number of weak learners and the regulariz
ation term helps control the complexity of the learners.
 It is important to note that the above specification of the regularization
 term is only one of many possible specifications, though 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "chen2016xgboost"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 claim that it is simpler than the leading alternatives and easier to paralleliz
e.
 Further note that when the hyperparameters are set to zero, the objective
 function becomes equivalent to that used in traditional gradient tree boosting.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To minimize the objective function, the model is trained in a sequential
 manner, building one tree at a time.
 The objective function at iteration 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 can be written as follows: 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}_{t}=\sum_{i}l(y_{i},\hat{y}_{i}^{t-1}+f_{t}(x_{i}))+r(f_{t})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{y}_{i}^{t}=\hat{y}_{i}^{t-1}+f_{t}(x_{i})$
\end_inset

.
 Each iteration then adds the tree 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{t}(x_{i})$
\end_inset

 that greedily minimizes the objective function at that iteration.
 More specifically, EGB uses a second-order Taylor series expansion to approxima
te the loss function at any given iteration:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\tilde{\mathcal{L}}_{t}=\sum_{i}\left\{ g(y_{i},\hat{y}_{i}^{t-1})f_{t}(x_{i})+\frac{1}{2}h(y_{i},\hat{y}_{i}^{t-1})f_{t}(x_{i})^{2}\right\} +r(f_{t})\label{eq:approx}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $g(\cdot,\cdot)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $h(\cdot,\cdot)$
\end_inset

 denote the first- and second-order derivatives of the loss function 
\begin_inset Formula $l(y_{i},\hat{y}_{i}^{t-1})$
\end_inset

 with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{y}_{i}^{t-1}$
\end_inset

.
 That is, the point 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{y}_{i}^{t-1}$
\end_inset

 is taken as the basis for the expansion.
 The above conveniently omits the first term of the series 
\begin_inset Formula $l(y_{i},\hat{y}_{i}^{t-1})$
\end_inset

 because it is a constant.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The EGB algorithm is based on a re-expression of Eq.
\begin_inset space \thinspace{}
\end_inset

(
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:approx"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

).
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{j}$
\end_inset

 denote the set of observations that belong to the 
\begin_inset Formula $j^{th}$
\end_inset

 leaf of the tree being built at iteration 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
 Substituting in the explicit form of the regularization term and noting
 that all observation that belong to the same leaf receive the same prediction
 
\begin_inset Formula $m_{tj}$
\end_inset

, we can write 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\tilde{\mathcal{L}}_{t}=\sum_{j}\sum_{i\in s_{j}}\left\{ g(y_{i},\hat{y}_{i}^{t-1})m_{tj}+\frac{1}{2}h(y_{i},\hat{y}_{i}^{t-1})m_{tj}^{2}\right\} +\gamma M_{t}+\frac{1}{2}\lambda\sum_{j}m_{tj}^{2}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

or, equivalently,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\tilde{\mathcal{L}}_{t}=\sum_{j}\left\{ G_{j}m_{tj}+\frac{1}{2}(H_{j}+\lambda)m_{tj}^{2}\right\} +\gamma M_{t}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $G_{j}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{j}$
\end_inset

 represent the sums over 
\begin_inset Formula $g(\cdot,\cdot)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $h(\cdot,\cdot)$
\end_inset

 for all observations in 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{j}$
\end_inset

.
 Note that the summation in the above is over 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

, which indexes the leaves in the tree.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For a given tree structure, we can find the optimal prediction values by
 minimizing the objective function with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $m_{tj}$
\end_inset

, which yields 
\begin_inset Formula $m_{tj}^{*}=-G_{j}/(H_{j}+\lambda)$
\end_inset

.
 With a squared-error loss function, where 
\begin_inset Formula $g(y_{i},\hat{y}_{i}^{t-1})=-2(y_{i}-\hat{y}_{i}^{t-1})$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $h(y_{i},\hat{y}_{i}^{t-1})=2$
\end_inset

, the optimal prediction values are effectively the (penalized) average
 of the residuals associated with a given leaf.
 We can then substitute the optimal prediction values into the objective
 function to obtain the following: 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\tilde{\mathcal{L}}_{t}^{*}=-\frac{1}{2}\sum_{j}\frac{G_{j}^{2}}{H_{j}+\lambda}+\gamma M_{t}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

which gives us the minimal objective function for a given tree structure.
 Ideally, one would enumerate all possible tree structures and then use
 the tree associated with the smallest value of the (minimized) objective
 function, but this is often intractable.
 EGB instead implements an alternative approach that seeks to minimize the
 objective at a given iteration by greedily optimizing one level of the
 tree at a time.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To this end, consider splitting a node in a given tree into 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

left
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

right
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 child nodes based on some candidate splitting rule.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $G_{L}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $G_{R}$
\end_inset

 represent 
\begin_inset Formula $G_{j}$
\end_inset

 for the left and right child nodes, respectively.
 Similarly, let 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{L}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{R}$
\end_inset

 represent 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{j}$
\end_inset

 for the child nodes.
 We can then find the reduction in the objective function as follows: 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\Delta\tilde{\mathcal{L}}_{t}^{*}=\frac{1}{2}\left\{ \frac{G_{L}^{2}}{H_{L}+\lambda}+\frac{G_{R}^{2}}{H_{R}+\lambda}-\frac{(G_{L}+G_{R})^{2}}{H_{L}+H_{R}+\lambda}\right\} -\gamma\label{eq:gain}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where the first two terms in parentheses capture the loss associated with
 the child nodes and the third term captures the loss associated with the
 parent node being split.
 When splitting a node in any given tree, EGB then evaluates Eq.
\begin_inset space \thinspace{}
\end_inset

(
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:gain"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

) for every possible split rule for every available explanatory variable
 and chooses the split associated with the maximal reduction in the objective
 function.
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
This is known as the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

exact greedy algorithm.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 EGB also has an approximate algorithm that can be used for large datasets.
 See 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "chen2016xgboost"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 for details.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 EGB then continues to split nodes in a given tree until a stopping criterion
 is met (e.g., a user-specified maximum tree depth).
 Once a given tree is built, EGB then moves to building the next tree in
 the ensemble and continues to do so until reaching the user-specified number
 of trees to build.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The above references several hyperparameters that must be chosen by the
 user, including the hyperparameters in the regularization term (i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset

), the maximum tree depth, and the number of trees in the ensemble.
 There are two additional hyperparameters that can be used to further prevent
 overfitting.
 First, one can use column or feature subsampling where each tree in the
 ensemble is built based on a (uniformly) random subset of all explanatory
 variables.
 This procedure can also speed up the algorithm because it reduces the number
 of covariates that must be searched across when creating new splits.
 Second, one can use shrinkage by scaling the predictions associated with
 each tree by a factor 
\begin_inset Formula $\eta$
\end_inset

, which is often called the learning rate.
 The predicted value of the outcome at iteration 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 is then given by 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{y}_{i}^{t}=\hat{y}_{i}^{t-1}+\eta f_{t}(x_{i})$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Note Note
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
We're using iteration and tree for t, I presume this is correct but wanted
 to be sure.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 Shrinkage reduces the influence of each tree so that no individual tree
 has too much influence on the ensemble prediction.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
One possible approach to hyperparameter selection is to use a grid search
 where the user (1) specifies the domain of the hyperparameters, (2) applies
 the model to every combination of hyperparameters, and (3) selects the
 hyperparameters that perform the best in terms of some metric (e.g., mean
 squared prediction error in the validation dataset).
 Grid search, however, is computationally inefficient because hyperparameter
 proposals are uninformed by previous evaluations, meaning that the procedure
 spends considerable time evaluating suboptimal hyperparameters.
 We instead use a Bayesian hyperparameter optimization procedure, which
 sequentially updates a probability model that relates the hyperparameter
 space to the performance metric and then uses the model to intelligently
 explore the hyperparameter space 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "bergstra2011algorithms"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Specifically, our implementation of gradient boosting relies on the open-source
 library XGBoost, which we combine with the Hyperopt library that conducts
 Bayesian hyperparameter optimization using the tree-structured Parzen estimator.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Note Comment
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
