\begin_layout Subsubsection
Validation methods for machine learning based estimates
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:Validation-methods-for"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The coefficient of determination or 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 is the most commonly used metric for validating poverty maps generated
 using machine-learning methods.
 The standard validation procedure can be summarized in four steps: (1)
 using survey data calculate a poverty measure for the geographic unit of
 interest (e.g., village or cluster) and merge the poverty measure with features
 from some passively-collected data source (e.g., mobile phone data or satellite
 imagery); (2) randomly split the dataset into training and testing subsamples;
 (3) fit a machine-learning model to the training data and use the model
 to predict poverty in the testing data; (4) calculate the out-of-sample
 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 by regressing the observed poverty measures from the testing data on the
 predicted poverty measures for the same geographic units.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Rather than relying on a single training-testing split of the data, many
 studies use cross-validation and report the average 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 across various training-testing splits.
 For examples of the above procedure, see 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "jean2016combining"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "pokhriyal2017combining"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "steele2017mapping"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "lee2020high"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "yeh2020using"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, or 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "chi2022microestimates"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Note that a few studies use a similar procedure, but fit the machine-learning
 model to household-level data and then aggregate the model's predictions
 to the geographic unit of interest 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "blumenstock2015predicting,hersh2021open,aiken2022machine"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 It is important to emphasize that the poverty measures used in this procedure
 are often direct, sample-based estimates rather than the true poverty measures
 for the regions of interest.
 In what follows, we critically assess this validation procedure and discuss
 some alternative validation metrics.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{a}$
\end_inset

 denote the actual or true poverty indicator for the 
\begin_inset Formula $i^{th}$
\end_inset

 geographical entity.
 Further, let 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{d}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{p}$
\end_inset

 denote the direct estimate and model-based prediction of the poverty indicator,
 respectively.
 The standard method for assessing the predictive performance of model-based
 poverty estimates calculates the 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 from a regression like the following:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{i}^{d}=\alpha+\beta y_{i}^{p}+\varepsilon_{i}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 are the regression coefficients and 
\begin_inset Formula $\varepsilon$
\end_inset

 represents the error term.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Note that any regression of this form can only be estimated for the sampled
 regions with direct estimates.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 The 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 for the above regression can be written as follows:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
R_{d}^{2}=\frac{\sigma_{d}^{2}-\sigma_{\varepsilon}^{2}}{\sigma_{d}^{2}}\label{eq:r2direct}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{d}^{2}$
\end_inset

 is the variance of the direct estimate and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{\varepsilon}^{2}$
\end_inset

 is the variance of 
\begin_inset Formula $\varepsilon$
\end_inset

.
 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{d}^{2}$
\end_inset

 thus conveys information about how much of the variance of the direct estimate
 is explained by the model-based prediction.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
While unbiased, direct estimates can be imprecise estimates of true poverty
 indicators 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "corral2020pull"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Ideally, one would assess the performance of model-based predictions on
 the basis of the true poverty indicators (
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{a}$
\end_inset

) themselves.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
That is, model-based predictions are more appropriately assessed using the
 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 from the following regression:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{i}^{a}=\alpha+\beta y_{i}^{p}+e_{i}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $e$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Note Note
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
I changed it from eta because we use eta to denote the random location effect
 in SAE
\end_layout

\end_inset

represents the error term.
 The 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 for this regression can written as
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
R_{a}^{2}=\frac{\sigma_{a}^{2}-\sigma_{e}^{2}}{\sigma_{a}^{2}}\label{eq:r2true}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{a}^{2}$
\end_inset

 is the variance of the true poverty indicator and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{e}^{2}$
\end_inset

 is the variance of 
\begin_inset Formula $e$
\end_inset

.
 We are specifically interested in characterizing the bias of 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{d}^{2}$
\end_inset

 when it is used to assess true quantity of interest, 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{a}^{2}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{d}=y_{i}^{a}+\omega_{i}$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega$
\end_inset

 is a mean-zero error term with variance 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{\omega}^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 That is, we view this as a classical measurement error problem where the
 direct estimate of the poverty indicator is a noisy estimate of the true
 poverty indicator.
 Note that the preceding equation implies that 
\begin_inset Formula $\varepsilon_{i}=e_{i}+\omega_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{\varepsilon}^{2}=\sigma_{e}^{2}+\sigma_{\omega}^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Following 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "majeske2010quantifying"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, we then define the bias as:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
B=R_{a}^{2}-R_{d}^{2}.\label{eq:bias}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Substituting Eqs.
\begin_inset space \thinspace{}
\end_inset

(
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:r2direct"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

) and (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:r2true"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

) into our expression for the bias, we have:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
B=\frac{\sigma_{a}^{2}-\sigma_{e}^{2}}{\sigma_{a}^{2}}-\frac{\sigma_{d}^{2}-\sigma_{e}^{2}-\sigma_{\omega}^{2}}{\sigma_{d}^{2}}=\frac{\sigma_{\omega}^{2}(\sigma_{a}^{2}-\sigma_{e}^{2})}{\sigma_{d}^{2}\sigma_{a}^{2}}=\frac{\sigma_{\omega}^{2}}{\sigma_{d}^{2}}R_{a}^{2}.\label{eq:bias_res}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Finally, we can substitute Eq.
\begin_inset space \thinspace{}
\end_inset

(
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:bias_res"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

) into Eq.
\begin_inset space \thinspace{}
\end_inset

(
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:bias"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

) and rearrange, which gives
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
R_{d}^{2}=R_{a}^{2}\left(\frac{\sigma_{d}^{2}-\sigma_{\omega}^{2}}{\sigma_{d}^{2}}\right)\label{eq:relationship}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and establishes a basic relationship between 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{d}^{2}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{a}^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 See 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "majeske2010quantifying"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 for additional mathematical details.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In Eq.
\begin_inset space \thinspace{}
\end_inset

(
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:relationship"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

) above, we see that 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{d}^{2}=R_{a}^{2}$
\end_inset

 when 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{\omega}^{2}=0$
\end_inset

 or, equivalently, when 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{d}=y_{i}^{a}$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

.
 In the presence of measurement error or when 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{\omega}^{2}>0$
\end_inset

, we then see that 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{d}^{2}$
\end_inset

 will be biased downward and the magnitude of the bias will be determined
 by the size of 
\begin_inset Formula $(\sigma_{d}^{2}-\sigma_{\omega}^{2})/\sigma_{d}^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Validation exercises based on 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{d}^{2}$
\end_inset

 will thus tend to mischaracterize the performance of model-based approaches
 to poverty mapping, with the resulting estimates of 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 being biased downward (see Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:standard_implementation"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

 for estimates of the bias).
 One implication of the above is that 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{d}^{2}$
\end_inset

 is context-dependent and will generally be influenced by the design of
 the survey data (e.g., larger sample sizes will tend to reduce measurement
 error, all else equal).
 In particular, a poorly performing model applied to a low bias setting
 may yield an estimate of 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{d}^{2}$
\end_inset

 that is higher than an estimate of 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{d}^{2}$
\end_inset

 from a strong model applied to a high bias setting.
\begin_inset Note Note
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Shouldn't this be tied to measurement error, instead of bias? That is, link
 it to the measurement error, since the direct estimates won't be biased.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 The presence of bias
\begin_inset Note Note
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
I think it's best to frame it around sampling error
\end_layout

\end_inset

 in the measures of 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 can thus reverse the rank ordering of models when comparisons are made
 across contexts.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
See 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "yeh2020using"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "chi2022microestimates"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, or 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "aiken2022machine"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 for this type of cross-context comparison of 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 values.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
There is an additional form of bias in 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 that can lead to overoptimistic conclusions related to model validity.
 To illustrate this, note that 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{a}^{2}$
\end_inset

 in Eq.
\begin_inset space \thinspace{}
\end_inset

(
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:r2true"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

) is mathematically equivalent to the squared (Pearson) correlation coefficient
 between 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{a}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{p}$
\end_inset

.
 A well-known property of the correlation coefficient is that it is invariant
 to any positive affine transformation of its arguments, implying that the
 squared correlation coefficient is invariant to any affine transformation
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "gujarati2003basic"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
More specifically, the correlation coefficient is invariant to any 
\shape italic
positive
\shape default
 affine transformation because it changes signs when one of its arguments
 is scaled by some negative constant.
 The 
\shape italic
squared
\shape default
 correlation coefficient is then invariant to any affine transformation.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 We can thus write
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
R_{a}^{2}(y_{i}^{a},y_{i}^{p})=R_{a}^{2}(y_{i}^{a},c_{1}+c_{2}y_{i}^{p})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{2}$
\end_inset

 are some arbitrary constants.
 The implication of the above is that it implies that the 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 is insensitive to systematic bias in the model-based poverty estimates.
 For example, consider some unbiased poverty estimate 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{u}$
\end_inset

, meaning that 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{i}^{u}-y_{i}^{a})=0.$
\end_inset

 Now consider another estimate 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{b}$
\end_inset

 that is identical to 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{u}$
\end_inset

 up to some locational shift, implying that 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{b}=y_{i}^{u}+c$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{i}^{b}-y_{i}^{a})=c$
\end_inset

.
 While 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{b}$
\end_inset

 is biased (i.e., it will systematically over- or under-estimate poverty),
 it will achieve an 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 that is identical to 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{u}$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A final issue related to 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 as a performance metric is that it is affected by the variance of the true
 poverty measures.
 To see this, consider the following re-expression of 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{a}^{2}$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
R_{a}^{2}=1-\frac{\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i}(y_{i}^{a}-\alpha-\beta y_{i}^{p})^{2}}{\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i}(y_{i}^{a}-\bar{{y}}^{a})^{2}}=1-\frac{\text{{MSE}}(y_{i}^{a},\alpha+\beta y_{i}^{p})}{\text{{Var}}(y_{i}^{a})}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 is the number of geographical entities.
 The above shows that 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 can be viewed as a normalized version of the mean squared error with the
 variance of the true poverty indicator used as the normalization factor.
 This approach to normalization produces an additional source of context-depende
nce in 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Specifically, consider two models that perform equally well in that they
 achieve the same mean squared error.
 However, assume that one model is applied to a context where the variance
 of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{a}$
\end_inset

 is large and the other is applied to a low variance context.
 The above then implies that the model applied to the high variance context
 will achieve a higher 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

, even though the performance of the two models is arguably identical.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Rather than relying on 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 as a performance metric, one can avoid regression-based model assessment
 altogether by calculating the mean squared error directly:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\text{{MSE}}(y_{i}^{a},y_{i}^{p})=\frac{{1}}{N}\sum_{i}(y_{i}^{a}-y_{i}^{p})^{2},\label{eq:mse}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

which avoids the normalization issue and provides a more direct understanding
 of the expected squared error of the estimates.
 The mean squared error in Eq.
\begin_inset space \thinspace{}
\end_inset

(
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:mse"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

) is also sensitive to systematic biases in the predictions.
 Reconsidering the biased and unbiased poverty estimates described above
 (i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{u}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{b}$
\end_inset

), we can write 
\begin_inset Formula $\text{{MSE}}(y_{i}^{b},y_{i}^{a})=\text{{MSE}}(y_{i}^{u}+c,y_{i}^{a})=\text{{MSE}}(y_{i}^{u},y_{i}^{a})+c^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Systematic biases in the estimates will thus be penalized by the direct
 mean squared error criterion.
 Further, with multiple estimates for each entity, one can calculate entity-spec
ific mean squared errors, which can be decomposed into parts associated
 with the bias and variance of the estimates.
 Where 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{ik}^{p}$
\end_inset

 denotes estimate 
\begin_inset Formula $k=1,2,\ldots,K$
\end_inset

 for the 
\begin_inset Formula $i^{th}$
\end_inset

 entity, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\text{{MSE}}_{i}=\frac{{1}}{K}\sum_{k}(y_{i}^{a}-y_{ik}^{p})^{2}=\frac{{1}}{K}\sum_{k}(y_{ik}^{p}-\bar{{y}}_{i}^{p})^{2}+(\bar{{y}}_{i}^{p}-y_{i}^{a})^{2}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where the first term on the right-hand side of the above captures the variance
 of the estimates for entity 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 and the second term captures the (squared) bias.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
See 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "hastie2009elements"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 for derivation and detailed discussion of the bias-variance decomposition.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Unit-level small area estimation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The purpose of this section is to give readers a brief introduction to the
 small area estimation methods applied in the proposed validation exercises.
 Opposite to machine learning methods, under unit-level based small area
 estimation there is an assumed underlying model for welfare generation.
 The model used under the EB method of Molina and Rao (2010) and ELL is
 a nested error model originally proposed by Battesse, Harter and Fuller
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citeyear
key "BHF"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, BHF).
 For the estimation of poverty and welfare, the ELL and EB methods assume
 the transformed welfare 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{ih}$
\end_inset

 for each household 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

 in the population within each location 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 is linearly related to a 
\begin_inset Formula $1\times K$
\end_inset

 vector of characteristics (or correlates) 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{ih}$
\end_inset

 for that household, according to the nested error model: 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{ih}=x_{ih}\beta+\eta_{i}+e_{ih},\:h=1,\ldots,N_{i},\,i=1,\ldots,I.\label{eq:1-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Here 
\begin_inset Formula $\eta_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{ih}$
\end_inset

 are respectively location and household-specific idiosyncratic errors,
 assumed to be independent from each other, satisfying: 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\eta_{i}\stackrel{iid}{\sim}N\left(0,\sigma_{\eta}^{2}\right),\:e_{ih}\stackrel{iid}{\sim}N\left(0,\sigma_{e}^{2}\right),
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where the variances 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{\eta}^{2}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{e}^{2}$
\end_inset

 are unknown.
 Here, 
\begin_inset Formula $I$
\end_inset

 is the number of locations in which the population is divided and 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{i}$
\end_inset

 is the number of households in location 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

, for 
\begin_inset Formula $i=1,\ldots,I$
\end_inset

.
 Finally, 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 is the 
\begin_inset Formula $K\times1$
\end_inset

 vector of coefficients.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
It is evident that the dependent variable of the model is not the poverty
 status of a given household.
 Instead, the method attempts to replicate the welfare distribution based
 on an assumed data generating process.
 The parameters obtained from the model fit using the household survey data
 are then applied to the census data, including simulated errors.
 From the simulated welfare vector in the census it is possible to obtain
 estimates of any welfare indicator, not just poverty headcount rates.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
One major aspect of concern for small area estimation is the estimation
 of noise.
 A statistical agency in a given country may seek to undertake a small area
 estimation application because the quality of the estimates derived from
 survey data may not be sufficiently precise or estimates may not even be
 possible since the area was not sampled.
 Hence, a big concern surrounding small area estimation is the estimation
 of MSEs for each area's estimate.
 Estimates of noise are obtained via bootstrap replications where the model's
 assumptions are exploited in order to obtain an estimated MSE.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
For a much more detailed discussion on the estimation of noise refer to
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "rao2015small"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 and for a discussion focused on ELL and EB estimates refer to 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "corral2020pull"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 This aspect is one of the biggest differences between poverty maps based
 on small area estimation and those based on machine learning.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A modification of the typical unit-level small area estimation approach
 presented above which allowed updating poverty maps even when the census
 and survey data are not aligned was presented by Nguyen (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citeyear
key "doi:10.1080/00220388.2012.682983"
literal "false"

\end_inset

).
 The basic idea behind the approach is that no household level covariates
 are used.
 This way there is no need for the covariates in the survey to match the
 moments of those from the census.
 Nguyen (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citeyear
key "doi:10.1080/00220388.2012.682983"
literal "false"

\end_inset

) introduces the variation of the ELL method using data from Vietnam and
 suggests that only area and cluster level covariates be used.
 A further refinement was introduced by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "masaki2020small"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 which relied on EB estimates.
 The method was tested and validated by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "corral2021map"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 using the same Mexican data illustrated here.
 Moreover, the method (referred to as unit-context) is thoroughly discussed
 and dissected in 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "corral2022guidelines"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 and shown to be flawed.
 However, since it does offer certain advantages, it is included here for
 the sake of comparison between methods which can be applied for off-census
 years.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Targeting efficiency of the methods
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
While understanding the noise of the poverty estimates is useful, the ultimate
 concern with poverty mapping is to understand how different methodological
 choices affect poverty alleviation.
 Similar to 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "elbers2007poverty"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, we propose simulating a hypothetical poverty-alleviation program that
 distributes resources to households according to a given poverty map.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
See 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "yeh2020using"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "chi2022microestimates"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "aiken2022machine"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 for examples of other studies that have conducted targeting simulations.
 
\end_layout

\end_inset

 In addition to the poverty estimates, the procedure applied here requires
 true household-level incomes from a census-type dataset.
 The procedure is as follows:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace -15bp
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\paragraph_spacing single
\noindent
Using the true household-level per capita incomes, calculate the poverty
 headcount and poverty gap for the country as a whole for some chosen poverty
 line.
 Using the poverty gap measure, determine the budget required to completely
 eradicate poverty.
 Calculate an individual-level transfer amount by dividing the total budget
 by the number of people living in poverty.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\paragraph_spacing single
\noindent
Using the true poverty rates for each municipality we order the geographical
 entities according to their estimated poverty headcounts, from most to
 least poor.
 For the poorest region, augment the per capita income of all household's
 by an amount equal to the transfer (i.e., each household receives an amount
 equal to the transfer times the number of household members).
 Continue this distribution procedure for the second poorest region, the
 third poorest region, and so on until there is not enough resources to
 cover all households in a given region.
 For the marginal region, distribute the remaining budget equally among
 individuals in that region.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\paragraph_spacing single
\noindent
Using the post-transfer household incomes, calculate an updated poverty
 headcount to assess the effects of the distribution scheme on poverty alleviati
on.
 Note that all of this is done with the census which has an income measure.
 This process yields the best case scenario for geographical targeting where
 transfers are given to the poorest locations, where poverty is determined
 by the headcount rate.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The procedure detailed above is then repeated with the estimates obtained
 under each method.
 The rankings are determined by the estimates obtained under a given method,
 but the transfer amount is then applied to the true income measure in the
 census to calculate the impact of the targeted transfer.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The procedure applied differs from the ideal transferring scheme detailed
 by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "elbers2007poverty"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, since under the different methods all we have estimated are poverty headcount
 rates.
 Moreover, the ideal transfer proposed by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "elbers2007poverty"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 is not simple to illustrate to policy makers and is seldom implemented.
 The process detailed here in reality mimics how many governments determine
 which locations to give priority for a government cash transfer scheme.
 For example, in Ghana, the Livelihood Empowerment Against Poverty Programme’s
 (LEAP) main objective is to reduce poverty by increasing consumption and
 promoting access to services and opportunities among the extreme poor and
 vulnerable.
 The program provides social cash grants to extremely poor and vulnerable
 households.
 The latest poverty map for Ghana is used as an input to rank districts
 and communities before selecting communities where the program will collect
 data to identify extreme poor households.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Another example is that of Peru.
 In Peru, small area estimates of poverty are used to determine areas that
 should be prioritized in the allocation of public funds.
 The Ministry of Finance of Peru uses the estimates to determine allocations
 to programs.
 One such program is called “Programa de Vaso de Leche” that offers a daily
 food ration to vulnerable populations, particularly children under 13 and
 pregnant mothers, with the goal of reducing food insecurity.
 Another program that makes use of small area estimates of poverty in Peru
 is the “Canon Minero” which is a program that ensures that local and regional
 governments receive funds obtained by the State from income taxes collected
 from mining activities.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
During the COVID-19 crisis in Nigeria, poverty estimates at a granular geographi
cal level were used to determine how to allocate funds from the Federal
 Government’s COVID-19 Rapid Response Registration (RRR) Cash Transfer Project
 to 1 million Nigerians.
 The RRR selected the poorest wards in the country based on the poverty
 estimates at the ward level, something that would be unlikely with the
 National Living Standards Survey (NLSS) which is only representative at
 the State level.
 Additionally, many wards in the country are not even present in the NLSS,
 consequently the need for model-based approaches toward obtaining poverty
 estimates.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace -5bp
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Data
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The data used here was originally created for the small area validation
 illustrated in 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "corral2021map"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 and thus the section borrows heavily from there.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In this section we present a design-based simulation experiment based on
 the Mexican Intra Censal Survey of 2015 (Encuesta Intracensal).
 The purpose of the simulation is to observe performance of the different
 methods under more realistic scenarios.
 The survey is carried out by the Mexican National Institute of Statistics
 and Geography (Instituto Nacional de Estadistica y Geografia - INEGI).
 The survey has a sample of 5.9 million households and is representative
 at the national, state (32 states) and municipal or delegation level (2,457
 municipalities), as well as for localities with a population of 50,000
 or more inhabitants.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The 2015 Intra Censal Survey questionnaire includes the following topics
 related to the housing unit: dwelling features, size and use of the dwelling,
 conditions for cooking, ownership and access conditions, access to water,
 sanitation facilities and sanitation, electric power, solid waste, equipment,
 appliances and automobile; and information and communication technologies
 (ICT).
 It also includes the following demographic information: total population
 and structure, birth registration, marital status, health services, ethnicity,
 education, economic characteristics, nonpaid work, migration, daily mobility,
 fertility and mortality, household composition, non-labor household income,
 food security, agricultural land use, relationship to the household head,
 indigenous language, occupation, economic activity, and accumulated education.
 The survey also includes indicators for states, municipalities, and counties.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
One of the key features of the survey is its size and the fact that it includes
 a measure of income at the household level.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Income is defined as money received from work performed during the course
 of the reference week by individuals of age 12 or older within the household.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 The inclusion of an income measure allows for a design-based validation
 of the different methods presented above.
 The next section describes how the survey is modified to create a census
 data set and how samples are then drawn from the created census data with
 the goal of obtaining small area estimates of poverty at the municipal
 level.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Creating a census and survey
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:Creating-a-census"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Because the goal of this exercise is to test how well the different methods
 perform under a real world scenario, the Intra Censal Survey is modified
 to mimic a Census in order to allow for a design-based simulation.
 The first step consists of randomly removing 90 percent of households that
 reported an income of 0.
 This is done to ensure that some households with an income of 0 are included
 in the population, but not as many as in the original data to make it more
 realistic.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Welfare values of 0 and/or missing are a common feature of household surveys.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 In the second step, all municipalities with less than 500 households are
 removed, thus observations by municipality range from 501 to 23,513.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
The median municipality has 1,613 households.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 The final 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Census
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 consists of 3.9 million households and 1,865 municipalities.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To draw survey samples, primary sampling units (PSU) need to be created.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
The Intra Censal Survey has its own PSUs, however many of these have too
 few observations to properly work as PSUs in the created 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Census
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 data.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 Within each municipality, the original data's PSUs are sorted
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
This assumes that the clusters' numbering is tied to how proximate each
 cluster is to one and other.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 and joined until each created PSU has close to 300 households.
 Under the proposed approach, original PSUs are never split, just joined
 to others.
 Additionally, all original PSUs that were larger than 300 households are
 designated as a created PSU.
 The entire process yields 16,297 PSUs.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The resulting Census data is used to draw 500 survey samples to conduct
 a design-based simulation to establish how a method will perform over repeated
 sampling from a finite population (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citealp
key "tzavidis2018start"
literal "false"

\end_inset

).
 The sampling approach reflects standard designs used in most face-to-face
 household surveys conducted in the developing world, such as those conducted
 by the Living Standards Measurement Study (LSMS) program of the World Bank
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "grosh1996manual"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, with some simplification for convenience.
 First, the 32 states that comprise Mexico are treated as the intended domains
 of the sample.
 The main indicator of interest for the survey is the welfare measure: household
 per capita income.
 The desired level of precision to be achieved in the sample is assumed
 to be a relative standard error (
\begin_inset Formula $RSE$
\end_inset

) of 7.5 percent for mean per capita income in each state.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
The desired precision of an RSE of 7.5 percent is somewhat arbitrary but
 corresponds to precision targets in similar surveys and yields a sample
 of reasonable size.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 A two-stage clustered design is assumed with clusters (defined above) serving
 as primary sampling units (PSUs) selected in the first stage within each
 domain and then a sample of 10 households within each cluster is selected
 in the second stage.
 With these design features established, the trimmed census data was analyzed
 to identify the parameter estimates for per capita income (mean and standard
 deviation) for each state and the target standard error implied by the
 parameter estimates that corresponds to an 
\begin_inset Formula $RSE$
\end_inset

 of 7.5 percent.
 The minimum sample size required for state 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

, given these parameters, under simple random sampling (SRS) design is then
 obtained as: 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
n_{s}^{SRS}=\left(\frac{\sigma_{s}}{\bar{y}_{s}\times RSE^{tgt}}\right)^{2}=\left(\frac{\sigma_{s}}{\bar{y}_{s}\times0.075}\right)^{2}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{s}$
\end_inset

 is the standard deviation of per capita income in state 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $RSE^{tgt}$
\end_inset

 is the target standard error of 7.5 percent in state 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{y}_{c}$
\end_inset

 is the mean per capita income in state 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

.
 The minimum sample size under SRS must then be adjusted to account for
 the clustered design.
 This design effect due to clustering is accounted for by estimating the
 intra-cluster correlation (
\begin_inset Formula $ICC$
\end_inset

) for per capita income within each state using the trimmed census data.
 The 
\begin_inset Formula $ICC$
\end_inset

 estimates can then be applied to the SRS size obtained above to arrive
 at the minimum sample size for state 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

 under the clustered design employed here, given by:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
n_{s}=n_{s}^{SRS}\times DEFF_{s}=n_{s}^{SRS}\times\left(1+\left(n_{psu}-1\right)\rho_{s}\right)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $DEFF_{s}$
\end_inset

 is the design effect in state 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $n_{psu}$
\end_inset

 is the number of households selected within each cluster (10 in this case),
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho_{s}$
\end_inset

 is the 
\begin_inset Formula $ICC$
\end_inset

 for per capita income in state 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

.
 The minimum number of clusters to achieve 
\begin_inset Formula $n_{s}$
\end_inset

 (assuming 10 households per cluster) was calculated and then the final
 (household) sample size established by multiplying the number of clusters
 by 10.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Full estimates and results for the sample size determination are available
 upon request.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Taking the sample size requirements from above as fixed, the sample in each
 simulation is selected in accordance with the two-stage design.
 PSUs within each state, referred to here as clusters, are selected with
 probability proportional to size (
\begin_inset Formula $PPS$
\end_inset

) without replacement, where the measure of size is the number of households
 within the cluster.
 Then 10 households were selected within each cluster via simple random
 sampling.
 According to this design, the inclusion probability for household 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

 in cluster 
\begin_inset Formula $c$
\end_inset

 and in state 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

 is approximated
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
The equation used to calculate inclusion probabilities assumes sampling
 with replacement but is used here as an approximation of inclusion probabilitie
s under PPS selection without replacement.
 This should provide a reasonable approximation in this case since there
 are a relatively large number of PSUs present in the frame.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 as: 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p_{sch}=\frac{N_{sc}C_{s}}{N_{s}}\times\frac{n_{sc}}{N_{sc}}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{s}$
\end_inset

 is the total number of households in the census for state 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{sc}$
\end_inset

 is the number of households in cluster 
\begin_inset Formula $c$
\end_inset

 from state 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $C_{s}$
\end_inset

 is the number of clusters selected in state 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $n_{sc}$
\end_inset

 is the number of households selected in cluster 
\begin_inset Formula $c$
\end_inset

 within state 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

, which is fixed at 10.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
The design weight for each household is simply the inverse of the inclusion
 probability.
 In a typical survey, the design weights would be further adjusted for nonrespon
se and calibrated to known population characteristics.
 However, since the sampling is only a simulation exercise, there is no
 nonresponse and thus no nonresponse adjustment is required.
 Calibration or post-stratification could be performed but was not implemented
 to simplify the process.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The sample size across the 500 samples is roughly 23,540.
 Under the proposed sampling scenario, not all municipalities are included,
 and the number of municipalities included varies from sample to sample,
 ranging between 951 to 1,020 municipalities.
 The median municipality included in a given sample, is represented by a
 sole PSU and thus its sample size is of 10 households.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
The standard implementation
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:standard_implementation"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The objective of this section is to gauge how well the machine learning
 and small area estimation based poverty maps perform.
 Performance here is measured by bias and MSE.
 Using each of the 
\begin_inset Formula $S=500$
\end_inset

 survey samples drawn in section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:Creating-a-census"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

 we produce headcount poverty estimates (
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\tau}$
\end_inset

) for each municipality using each of the methods.
 The empirical design MSE for each municipality (
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

) under each method (
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset

) is measured as: 
\begin_inset Formula $MSE_{i}^{m}=\frac{1}{S}\sum_{s=1}^{S}\left(\hat{\tau}_{is}^{m}-\tau_{i}\right)^{2}$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau_{i}$
\end_inset

 is municipality 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

's true poverty rate obtained from the census created in section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:Creating-a-census"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

.
 The empirical design bias is then measured as 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{i}^{m}=\frac{1}{S}\sum_{s=1}^{S}\left(\hat{\tau}_{is}^{m}-\tau_{i}\right)$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
An additional variation to usual design based simulations is done here.
 In order to keep methods comparable, under each sample a different model
 is selected, whereas under traditional design based simulations the model
 is kept fixed across all samples.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As noted in section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:Validation-methods-for"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

, the 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 measure often used to assess the quality of machine learning based estimates
 is downward biased.
 Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:R2"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

 presents the 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 obtained in all 500 sets of estimates.
 The true 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 estimates across methods are quite similar with CensusEB estimates yielding
 the highest values.
 Nevertheless, if we were to assess these models using the direct estimates
 (i.e.
 what is normally done since true estimates are not available) we would
 come away with a different perception about the quality of these estimates.
 The median 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 with direct estimates is lowest for XGboost estimates and highest for unit-cont
ext models.
 Consequently, under the 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

 metric often used to evaluate models we would pick the model which in reality
 is the worst performing.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Assessing models based on their MSE and bias is usually preferable.
 This is because under small area estimation the model's overall performance
 is not necessarily the best reflection of the method's prowess.
 Since the goal is to obtain relatively accurate estimates of poverty for
 
\emph on
each
\emph default
 area we assess at the municipality level the bias and MSE of the different
 methods.
 Figures 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:Mean-squared-error "
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:Bias-across-municipalities"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

 present the MSE and bias of each method's municipality level estimates.
 The box-plots show that under the Mexican data, the machine learning approach
 of XGboost does a very solid job with an empirical MSE which rivals and
 maybe supersedes the one of CensusEB estimates.
 XGboost estimates are also far superior to those obtained under unit-context
 models.
 The empirical bias of CensusEB and XGboost methods is also closely aligned,
 suggesting that under the Mexican data used either method could be chosen.
 Also, evident is the fact that there is a downward bias in the unit-context
 models.
 The downward bias of the method is due to it not properly replicating the
 welfare distribution.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
A much richer discussion of the unit-context method can be found in 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "corral2021map,corral2022guidelines"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 
\end_layout

